    text: "Herodotus, writing about 440 BCE, divided the peoples of Scythia into nomads, royal Scythians and 'Scythian ploughmen' and 'farmers' who grew grain along the Dnieper and Bug, not to eat but to sell. Archaeology agrees: the forest-steppe hillforts such as Bilsk, the largest fortified settlement in Iron Age Europe, are ringed by fields and full of grain pits, sickles and quern stones. Wheat, barley and millet were sown with a wooden ard pulled by oxen, reaped in July and threshed on beaten floors. Much of the surplus went down the rivers to the Greek port of Olbia and on to Athens, which by the fourth century depended on Black Sea wheat.",
