    text: "Steppe nomads were never quite without fields. Mongol and Kazakh families, or poorer clients who stayed behind, sowed small plots of millet, barley or wheat near river bends at their winter or spring quarters, watering them from ditches, then moved off with the herds and came back to reap. Around Karakorum in the 1240s, captive Chinese and Central Asian farmers worked irrigated fields for the khans' court, a sight Plano Carpini and Rubruck both mention. Among the Kazakhs, farming spread in the nineteenth century as pasture shrank and poor herders called jataq settled; Kazakhs called the plot egin. Tilling was held lower than herding, work for those who had lost their animals.",
