    text: "The year of a Mongol or Kazakh herding family was a circuit between named pastures. Winter was spent in a sheltered valley or on the leeward side of hills where snow was thin; spring camps were where the animals gave birth; summer meant high or northern grass near water; autumn was fattening time before slaughter. Horses grazed furthest and were watched by young men; sheep and goats were driven out daily by boys and girls; camels and cattle browsed nearer. The great fear was the dzud, or zhut, when ice sealed the grass and whole herds starved. Pasture belonged to lineages by custom, and quarrels over wells and winter camps were the commonest cause of feuds.",
