    text: "Nomad government was personal: a khan, the chiefs of his lineages and a council, the kurultai, that met to choose rulers and decide on war. The Mongol empire added law officers called jarghuchi, who judged cases under the khan's decrees and kept registers, and a vast relay of post stations. Among the Kazakhs, justice was customary law, adat, spoken by biys, respected judges famed for eloquence, whose rulings were remembered in proverbs. Quarrels over animals, pasture, insults or women were settled by compensation, kun, a fixed price for injury or death counted in livestock. Refusing a biy's judgement could start a feud between whole clans, drawn out in raids called barymta.",
