    text: "In the early 1930s bread was rationed by card, and even after rationing ended in 1935 queues at state shops were part of every woman's day, though many Tashkent women now also went out to work. At home they still baked non in the courtyard tandyr, cooked plov on Fridays and for guests, and dried fruit for winter. Fuel was coal from the state depot, saxaul, and dried dung or cotton stalks in the villages. Money was short between kolkhoz settlements, so families borrowed from kin and neighbours and through rotating savings circles in which neighbours pooled money each month. Rationing returned in 1941, and the war years were hungry.",
