    title: "Red teahouses and loudspeaker radio",
    text: "The Soviet state reached neighbours through the places they already met. Teahouses, choyxona, where men gathered after work, were turned into 'red teahouses' with newspapers, posters and lecturers explaining the plan; new Uzbek papers like Qizil O'zbekiston, in a Latin alphabet from 1928 and Cyrillic from 1940, carried official news, and loudspeakers on poles broadcast radio in squares and kolkhozes. Real news still travelled by word of mouth, at the bazaar, the mosque, weddings and the courtyard gate, and neighbours in a mahalla knew each other's business closely. Denunciations passed the same way in 1937, and people learned to be careful what they said.",
