    text: "Tashkent in the 1930s was two cities: the old Uzbek city of winding lanes and courtyard houses of mud brick, and the Russian 'new city' of broad avenues, where the state built apartment blocks, offices and factories. Most Uzbek families lived in the old city or villages, where a house was built of sun-dried brick or rammed earth on a timber frame, and every spring roofs and walls were replastered with mud and straw, often through hashar, the neighbourly work party. Migrants flooded in from the countryside, and in the war years hundreds of thousands of evacuees and deportees arrived, squeezed into Uzbek homes and dormitories, in a city that would not stop growing.",
