    text: "Even party members were usually buried as Muslims. The body was washed, wrapped in white, carried on a bier to the cemetery and buried the same day after the janaza prayer, often led by an unofficial mullah; memorial meals with Qur'an recitation followed on the third, seventh and fortieth days and after a year, when the women of the family wailed and spoke of the dead. In the 1930s many families could not mourn openly for those shot or sent away in the purges. The Second World War left hundreds of thousands of Uzbek widows, who raised children on kolkhoz labour days and small pensions, with their husbands' families expected to support them.",
