    title: "Polyclinics and the tabib in Soviet Tashkent",
    text: "The 1930s brought state medicine to Tashkent on a large scale: polyclinics in each district, a medical institute training Uzbek doctors, and dental cabinets in factories and schools, where teeth were pulled or filled free of charge, if often without much anaesthetic. The state condemned the tabib and the mullah-healer as backward, but many families still went to them first, for a poultice of clove, a rinse of salt water, or a written amulet. In the villages a visiting feldsher, a medical assistant, might be the only trained help. Sugar rations and white bread were meanwhile making toothache more common than it had been for earlier generations.",
